 is directly linked to synonymous or near-synonymous terms, reinforcing that stakeholders may use different names for similar testing activities. Specifically, BIOMARKER TESTING is sometimes referred to as MOLECULAR TESTING, and precision oncology is described as another name framing the same biomarker-driven treatment-planning approach. [Data: Relationships (2568, 3700); Entities (2184)]\\n\\nThe relationships also connect MOLECULAR TESTING to GENOMIC TESTING and to TUMOR SEQUENCING, establishing a layered taxonomy: “biomarker/molecular/genomic” testing language describes the same overall objective (molecular characterization to guide care), while tumor sequencing is a key approach within that space. [Data: Relationships (2600, 2598); Entities (1625, 1623)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"The community emphasizes biomarker testing as a standard component of management in specific clinical scenarios (recurrent cervical cancer, advanced cancers, and rectal tumors)\",\n      \"explanation\": \"For RECURRENT CERVICAL CANCER, biomarker testing is explicitly recommended to guide treatment decisions and to identify clinical trial options, indicating that recurrence triggers reassessment of tumor characteristics and eligibility for biomarker-driven therapies (e.g., PD-L1 testing is mentioned in the disease description). [Data: Relationships (10718); Entities (4883)]\\n\\nIn ADVANCED CANCERS (later-stage, widely spread disease), biomarker and molecular testing are described as generally used to personalize systemic therapy choices, highlighting a broad, cross-cancer rationale for biomarker-driven treatment selection in high-stage disease. [Data: Relationships (2570); Entities (1586)]\\n\\nFor RECTAL TUMORS, the network specifies a concrete recommendation: all rectal tumors should undergo biomarker testing for mismatch repair deficiency (dMMR) or microsatellite instability-high (MSI-H). This shows how some tumor types have near-universal biomarker-testing expectations